    VALUES (subj, tid, 'Consider a continuous-time system with input $x(t)$ and output $y(t)$ given by $$y(t) = x(t)\cos(t)$$ This system is', 'Consider a continuous-time system with input $x(t)$ and output $y(t)$ given by
$$y(t) = x(t)\cos(t)$$
This system is
', '**Idea used:** test the two properties one by one.

1. **Linear?** Feed $a x_1 + b x_2$: the output is $(a x_1 + b x_2)\cos t = a\,x_1\cos t + b\,x_2\cos t$, which is $a y_1 + b y_2$. Yes, linear.
2. **Time-invariant?** Delay the input by $T$: the output is $x(t - T)\cos t$. But the delayed output would be $y(t - T) = x(t - T)\cos(t - T)$.
   These are not the same ($\cos t \ne \cos(t - T)$), so it is **time-varying**.

**Without a calculator:** multiplying the input by a fixed function of time (here $\cos t$, an AM modulator) is always linear,
and always time-varying, because the multiplier "knows" what time it is.

**Answer: (C).**
