    VALUES (subj, tid, 'A transistor circuit is given below. The Zener diode breakdown voltage is 5.3 V as shown. Take base to emitter voltage drop to be 0.6 V. The value of the...', 'A transistor circuit is given below. The Zener diode breakdown voltage is 5.3 V as shown. Take base to emitter voltage drop to be
0.6 V. The value of the current gain $\beta$ is _________.

![BJT with 220 ohm collector and 470 ohm emitter resistors; base held by a 5.3 V Zener fed from 10 V through 4.7 kilo-ohm; Zener current 0.5 mA](fig:q-gate2016-s1-q12)
', '**Idea used:** the Zener fixes the base voltage; from it get the emitter current, and from the 4.7 kΩ current get the base current.
Then $\beta = I_C / I_B$.

1. Base voltage $= 5.3$ V (the Zener holds it).
2. Emitter voltage $= 5.3 - 0.6 = 4.7$ V.
3. $I_E = 4.7 \text{ V} \div 470\ \Omega = 0.01$ A $= 10$ mA.
4. Current in the 4.7 kΩ: $(10 - 5.3) \text{ V} \div 4.7 \text{ k}\Omega = 4.7 \div 4.7 = 1$ mA.
5. At the base node this 1 mA splits: 0.5 mA goes into the Zener, so $I_B = 1 - 0.5 = 0.5$ mA.
6. $I_C = I_E - I_B = 10 - 0.5 = 9.5$ mA.
7. $\beta = 9.5 \div 0.5 = 19$.

**Check:** $I_E / I_B = 10 / 0.5 = 20 = \beta + 1$. ✔

**Without a calculator:** the numbers are set up to be easy: $4.7/470$ and $4.7/4.7$. Common slips: forgetting the 0.5 mA Zener
current (then $I_B = 1$ mA and $\beta = 9$), or forgetting the 0.6 V drop (then $\beta \approx 22$).

**Answer: 19** (GATE accepted 18 to 20).
', 'READY', 'NAT', 18, 20, 2, 'OFFICIAL_KEY', 'gate2016-s1-q12', 'PYQ') RETURNING id INTO qid;
