    INSERT INTO question(subject_id, topic_id, summary, stem_md, solution_md, status, answer_type, nat_min, nat_max,
                         difficulty, answer_status, content_key, origin)
    VALUES (subj, tid, 'The magnitude of three-phase fault currents at buses A and B of a power system are 10 pu and 8 pu, respectively. Neglect all resistances in the system and...', 'The magnitude of three-phase fault currents at buses A and B of a power system are 10 pu and 8 pu, respectively. Neglect all
resistances in the system and consider the pre-fault system to be unloaded. The pre-fault voltage at all buses in the system is
1.0 pu. The voltage magnitude at bus B during a three-phase fault at bus A is 0.8 pu. The voltage magnitude at bus A during a
three-phase fault at bus B, in pu, is ________.
', '**Idea used (bus impedance matrix):** for a fault at bus $k$, the fault current is $I_f = \dfrac{V_{pre}}{Z_{kk}}$ and the voltage at any
other bus $j$ drops to $V_j = V_{pre} - Z_{jk} I_f$. The network is passive, so $Z_{jk} = Z_{kj}$.

1. Fault at A: $I_A = 10$, so $Z_{AA} = 1 / 10 = 0.1$ pu.
2. Fault at B: $I_B = 8$, so $Z_{BB} = 1 / 8 = 0.125$ pu.
3. Fault at A, voltage at B: $0.8 = 1 - Z_{BA} \times 10$, so $Z_{BA} \times 10 = 0.2$ and $Z_{BA} = 0.02$ pu.
4. Fault at B, voltage at A: $V_A = 1 - Z_{AB} \times 8 = 1 - 0.02 \times 8 = 1 - 0.16 = 0.84$ pu.

**Without a calculator:** the drop at the far bus is (transfer impedance) × (fault current). The transfer impedance is shared, so
the drop scales with the fault current: $0.2 \times \dfrac{8}{10} = 0.16$, and $1 - 0.16 = 0.84$.
Option D is the drop itself; option A forgets that the fault current is different.

**Answer: 0.84 pu.**
', 'READY', 'NAT', 0.83, 0.85, 2, 'OFFICIAL_KEY', 'gate2016-s1-q20', 'PYQ') RETURNING id INTO qid;
